{
  "project": {
    "name": "OpenShip",
    "homepage": "https://openship.dev",
    "repository": "https://github.com/openshipdev/openship",
    "license": "MIT",
    "productDescription": "Discover open projects, understand how they work, and retrieve their verifiable source code.",
    "productSummary": "## What OpenShip does\n\nOpenShip helps people and agents understand and reproduce running software projects. Projects publish their source code and system design from their own origin.\n\nThe directory makes projects discoverable and keeps saved snapshots available for later exploration.\n\n### The problem it solves\n\nA running website rarely explains what powers it or which source files belong to its deployment. OpenShip provides a common discovery protocol, integrity-checked source bundles, and a viewer for code and architecture.",
    "technicalDescription": "Next.js and React app with a JavaScript protocol package, Postgres collection storage, and R2 source archives.",
    "technicalSummary": "## Implementation\n\nOpenShip uses Next.js App Router and React. The @openship/protocol package provides shared validators, TypeScript types, JSON schemas, and provider guidance. Source bundles are checked with SHA-256 digests.\n\n### Storage and collection\n\nPostgres with Drizzle stores project descriptions, Markdown summaries, collection jobs, and snapshot metadata. A private Cloudflare R2 bucket stores source archives. Better Auth handles authentication.\n\n### Deployment\n\nInstall dependencies with `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile`. Configure the variables documented in `docs/deployment.md`, including Postgres, R2, and authentication. Run `pnpm db:migrate`, then `pnpm build` and `pnpm start`, or deploy to Vercel with the collection cron configured in `vercel.json`."
  },
